[Music]  so I want to set the scene for what Jane  Jensen and Michelle then we'll discuss  by thinking about different conceptions  of inequality and what these different  conceptions of inequality might mean for  public policy and I'm going to argue in  favor of adopting more expansive  conceptions of inequality so we all know  that we live in an era of rising  inequality this figure draws on a famous  book by Thomas Piketty and the chart  shows the share of national income going  to people in the top one tenth of one  percent in Canada and on the right hand  side also the Gini index a well-known  measure of inequality my parents that's  my father were born in a moment of high  inequality I was born at a time when  inequality was falling I was born in  Montreal I was a very chubby baby and  many people here were born as I look  around somewhat later and of course just  at the time when inequality of various  sorts has been rising quite dramatically  but these facts alone this kind of chart  doesn't tell us as much as we want to  know about well-being or indeed about  how to improve well-being do the sums  that Galen Weston or Paul Desmarais earn  every year really matter to us I mean I  care more about whether Jane Jensen  earns more money than I do every year  but even that causes me only a small bit  of mental anguish  much more important than relative income  I think is absolute income namely the  incomes earned by people around the  median and you can see the arrow points  to this part of the diagram median  incomes in Canada have not varied very  dramatically over the last 30 years and  so here the news is not especially good  lower level incomes which is a bar just  have risen slightly more and that's good  news but though  poverty rates in Canada are falling they  remain alarmingly high as I think we all  know here especially among indigenous  people moreover the stagnation of median  income the stagnation of average income  is a reflection of deeper economic  problems problems that aren't going to  be easy to solve so we know for instance  that wages are tied to productivity but  productivity in Canada is rising much  less quickly than it is in the u.s. to  the south and for the first time in  decades a gap has opened up between the  rate of growth of productivity and the  rate of growth of wages I think this  diagram is one of the most important  that we can see today it means that  ordinary people are no longer securing  the fruits of their labor in the way  that they did for decades in this  country and in the u.s. the gap is  larger in the US but it's apparent and  present in Canada as well  now in order to understand the impact of  income inequality on well being I think  we also need to take an additional step  we need to realize that at the same time  that incomes are becoming more unequal  an increasing number of basic goods on  which people depend to maintain their  style of life a basic set of goods that  were once provided more or less free or  at low cost often provided as public  goods by government an increasing number  of those goods are now becoming  commodities for which  people are being charged and the prices  of those commodities are going up so  there's a kind of commoditization here  that's important college tuition is an  example this is Canadian college tuition  since 1992 ition in Canadian  universities has tripled  partly because governments are funding a  smaller share of the cost of tuition so  the burden borne by low-income families  in particular has risen dramatically and  of course education is especially  important because it's the means to rise  on the social ladder but the same thing  is true of home energy energy used to  heat and light the home has become  increasingly expensive in Canada over  the past 20 years more than a million  Canadians now spend more than 10 percent  of their income on heating energy light  alone so the result to use a kind of  technical term of social science is a  double whammy  just as incomes are becoming more  unequal access to basic goods is being  increasingly rationed by income and I  think this is something all of us who  study these phenomena need to think  about so what do these observations  imply for people concerned about social  justice concerned about social  well-being I think we can draw three  tentative conclusions here from this  part of what I want to say first  I think top incomes don't matter that  much what we should really be thinking  about is the trajectory of average  incomes the incomes of ordinary people  at the median and below the median  second to get this to work second I  think the solutions to inequality don't  lie entirely in redistribution by  governments that's what we normally  think about but we should also be  concerned about what Jacob hacker calls  predistribution  that is how the institutions of the  Canadian economy distribute market  come market income before taxes and  transfers and to some extent this turns  on government regulations regulations  about minimum wages regulations that  require firms to provide decent pension  Xand benefits but it also depends on  whether firms act in the interests of  their stakeholders as well as in the  interest of their shareholders and on  whether and that depends to some extent  on whether trade unions are strong  enough to pressure them to do so and on  that front the news isn't particularly  good here finally these observations  direct our attention I think to the  issue of what I'd call public provision  namely the extent to which governments  are willing to deliver public goods and  to deliver them at low cost I'm thinking  about sports facilities libraries  internet access public transport I mean  consider London England for instance  which where I usually am in the spring a  short ride on the London subway the tube  costs $9 Canadian these days in case any  of you were thinking of going there this  spring but at the same time  public transit buses and subway are free  for all London residents over the age of  60 think what a difference that makes  but think that that difference applies  to some people and not to others so  public provision in which Canada has  always been more successful in the u.s.  I might add is another key feature of  inequality and what we might be doing  about it okay so before closing I want  to take a second step I've got only 15  minutes here towards expanding our  conception of inequality and I want to  move beyond income to think about other  forms of inequality and how they impinge  on well-being so how might we do this  well one way to do this is to adopt what  members of the CFR successful societies  program have begun to call a  capabilities approach to well-being and  this is elaborated on our first book I  can only touch on it briefly here but I  want to say  something about it the basic idea is  that all people face a common set of  life challenges finding housing finding  a spouse caring for children finding a  job and the like and to these life  challenges every person brings a certain  set of capabilities so each person's  well-being depends to some extent on the  balance between the life challenges that  person faces and the capabilities the  person brings to those life challenges  so the question of interest for us of  course is where do those capabilities  come from and of course we know that  some of them reside in qualities of  character that are nurtured during  childhood and this means that the  circumstances for early childhood  development are very important in this  context what a person's capabilities are  also conditioned by our position within  a broader set of social and economic  structures a broader set of social and  economic relations and by virtue of this  her position in this set of economic  relations a person can call upon certain  economic resources for her capabilities  of course the classic example is income  incomes a multi-purpose instrument that  can be used as we all know to cope with  a variety of life challenges but  workplace autonomy also matters here the  security of employment also matters here  whether you can leave your job to take  care of a sick child makes a big  difference to your well-being and in  much the same way the members of this  program suggest that every person  occupies certain positions within a  parallel set of social structures that  provide that person with certain social  resources so these structures include  social networks namely ties to friends  and family  might help with finding a job might help  with finding housing or caring for a  child might provide emotional support in  the context of sickness and these  structures these social structures also  specify the social status that a person  enjoys and status is important because  people often call upon their status in  order to secure the cooperation of  others in life endeavors that's why I'm  wearing a tie today in order to  emphasize my social status so you'll  accept that answers I give to anybody's  questions and status is dependent to  some extent on the collective imaginary  of the community I can't say much about  this at the moment but the broad set of  narratives that tell us who belongs to  the community and what the members of  the community oh one another so the  basic idea should be pretty clear here  although it didn't isn't very clear in  my slide a person's well-being does not  simply depend on her place within the  distribution of income it also depends  on her place within a distribution of  social resources not just economic  resources but social resources and this  is important because research I've done  for the successful societies program  shows that social resources are also  distributed unequally across societies  this slide shows you people divided by a  standard set of social classes and it  gives you measures for the different  kinds of social ties in this case social  networks how dense are the social  networks that each person experiences  there's a kind of conventional wisdom  which says that well the wealthy can use  money to solve their problems the poor  don't have money but they use social  networks to solve their problems but  what this kind of figure suggests to you  is that in fact it's the wealthy who  have better dense or larger social  networks and those at lower levels of  income lack not only income but they  lack the social ties that are a critic  social resource let me close again by  asking what are the implications of this  somewhat broader capabilities  perspective on well-being what are the  implications for people concerned about  social justice and social well-being as  a whole well first we should all be  concerned not only about the  distribution of material resources but  about the distribution of social  resources namely about the access that  different kinds of people have to social  networks and the various kinds of status  conferred on different kinds of people  by the collective imaginaries of our  society the collective imaginary of  Quebec or of Canada and the successful  societies program has shown that these  resources impinge not only on people's  general well-being but also on their  health and if you want later on I can  show you some figures about that  secondly I think this observation  implies that the efforts of civic  organizations which many of you here  represent to build social networks among  the less affluent and to improve the  social status assigned to marginalized  communities like indigenous peoples  those efforts to build social networks  and to alter the status hierarchy may be  as important to overall well-being and  the well-being of the people you're  serving as efforts to increase the  material resources to those communities  this is not a substitute for material  resources but it's something we  shouldn't forget and finally these  observations have some important  implications for public policy  governments as we all know are often  fixated on the distribution of material  resources but public policies can also  enhance or erode the distribution of  social resources and governments often  do that as a byproduct an inadvertent  byproduct of policies aimed in other  direction  so if changes in a particular set of  zoning laws in one part of Montreal may  have some benefits maybe it in some ways  it contributes to the gentrification of  a community and maybe that has economic  benefits if those changes in zoning laws  eliminate fast-food restaurants in that  area some may see that as desirable but  that could well be as I well know from  my own community that could well  eliminate the gathering points at which  the elderly in that community get  together every morning and forged their  own social networks so well-meaning  policies described mainly in terms of  their material effects often have  unintended consequences with regard to  the distribution of social resources  labor regulations designed to improve  job mobility may well in some ways help  people secure jobs but at the same time  they may also isolate people take them  away from their ties to families and  friends that can be a very important  part of their social well-being in other  words we need to think about public  policy making in a somewhat new way as a  process of social resource creation we  need to recognize that public policies  can enhance and they can enrolled erode  they can alter the distribution of  social resources and policymakers should  be attentive I think to this side and as  citizens we should all be attentive to  this side of public policymaking we all  worry about the conservation of natural  resources we should also be worried  about the conservation of social  resources and I think that poses an  agenda of some importance for many  governments including governments in  Quebec thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

